From: Alexandr Mironov <7062352+alexandr-mironov@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] peripheral: do not pass a NULL range to diag_cntl_send_msg_mask()

The DM command DIAG_CMD_OP_SET_ALL_MSG_MASK (0x7D, op 5) ends in

    diag_cmd_set_all_msg_mask(req->mask);
    peripheral_broadcast_msg_mask(NULL);

and peripheral_broadcast_msg_mask() hands that NULL straight to
diag_cntl_send_msg_mask(), which dereferences it without a check as
soon as the mask status is ALL_ENABLED:

    diag_cmd_get_msg_mask(range, &mask);
    ...
    pkt->range = *range;

so the router dies with SIGSEGV on the first "enable all F3 messages"
request. Reproducible 100% with any DM client, e.g. sending
7D 05 00 FF FF FF FF over HDLC.

A NULL range here means "every range changed"; walk the whole table with
diag_cntl_send_masks() instead of dereferencing the pointer.

Tested on Xiaomi POCO X3 Pro (sm8150, mainline kernel, QRTR transport):
the router now survives the command, the masks reach the modem, and F3
messages start flowing.
---
--- a/router/peripheral.c
+++ b/router/peripheral.c
@@ -101,6 +101,15 @@
 	list_for_each(item, &peripherals) {
 		peripheral = container_of(item, struct peripheral, node);
 
-		diag_cntl_send_msg_mask(peripheral, range);
+		/*
+		 * A NULL range means "all masks changed at once", as issued by
+		 * DIAG_CMD_OP_SET_ALL_MSG_MASK. diag_cntl_send_msg_mask()
+		 * dereferences the range unconditionally, so walk the whole
+		 * table instead of handing it a NULL pointer.
+		 */
+		if (range)
+			diag_cntl_send_msg_mask(peripheral, range);
+		else
+			diag_cntl_send_masks(peripheral);
 	}
 }
